<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Purgatorio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Purgatorio is the second part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a>, written in the early 14th century. It describes Dante's climb, guided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, up the mountain of Purgatory, where souls are cleansed of their sins before entering Paradise. Many of the people Dante meets are his contemporaries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nino-visconti">Nino Visconti</a>, the last judge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purgatorio.jpg" alt="Purgatorio" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firenze,_commedia_di_dante,_codice_miniato_da_Simone_Camaldolese_e_aiuti,_purgatorio_canto_I,_1398,_tempi_1,_c._32r,_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Purgatorio follows the Inferno and precedes the Paradiso. Allegorically it represents the penitent Christian life, and in describing the climb <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> discusses the nature of sin, examples of vice and virtue, and moral questions of politics and the Church. Virgil is his guide until the last four cantos, where Beatrice takes his place. The poem holds that all sins arise from love: perverted love directed to others' harm, deficient love, or disordered and excessive love of good things. The journey begins on the shore of the mountain at 6 am on Easter Sunday 1300 and takes three and a quarter days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> places Purgatory on an island-mountain in the Southern Hemisphere, at the antipodes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, raised by rock displaced when Lucifer fell. Its lower slopes, which commentators call Ante-Purgatory, hold the excommunicate, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred of Sicily</a>, and the late-repentant, such as Belacqua and Pia de' Tolomei of Siena, murdered by her husband. Above them seven terraces purge the seven deadly sins: pride, envy, wrath, sloth, avarice, gluttony and lust. On each the souls meet examples of the opposite virtue, always including an episode from the life of the Virgin Mary, and on the terrace of lust every soul must pass through a wall of flame.</p><p>Prayer runs through the cantica: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> receives eleven requests for prayer, and Forese Donati has climbed most of the mountain within five years of his death through his wife Nella's prayers, while Statius has spent over 500 years there. At the summit lies the Earthly Paradise, where Matilda leads Dante to an allegorical procession, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> departs and Beatrice demands Dante's first personal confession of the Comedy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Scholars have long debated the size and shape of the mountain. In the Renaissance Antonio Manetti and Alessandro Vellutello proposed models that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> refuted, and later estimates put its height between 11.4 and 192 km. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s treatment of homosexual souls as capable of salvation was lenient for its time and is often left out of later illustrations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a>'s Symphony to Dante's Divina Commedia of 1856 has a Purgatorio movement, Chaucer referred to the poem, and Gustave Doré, John Flaxman, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-blake">William Blake</a> depicted its scenes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Purgatorio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dante quoted and alluded to it throughout the Divine Comedy and made Virgil his guide through the Inferno and <b>Purgatorio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The last judge, Nino Visconti, appears as a friend of Dante in the <b>Purgatorio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-gallura">Judicate of Gallura</a></li></ul></section>
